Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	## Behavior and review dispositions
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	`BeginTransaction()` creates an independent handle. Its collections, queries and readers stay bound across sequential thread handoff; ordinary calls remain independent. Overlapping use and callback reentry reject before execution, and completed/abandoned handles release session/application ownership.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The only integrated performance option is **Shared holder-worker and child `SharedEngine` wrapper reuse**. **Every transaction still releases native writer ownership and closes/reopens its storage core.** No coherent page cache survives an external writer. Plain/encrypted tests establish actual reuse, distinct core identity, external writer progress, refreshed indexed reads and cold reopen. Idle resources, coordination participation and mode-admission leases retire correctly; password/collation changes invalidate the wrapper. No group commit, batching, IPC service, retained native lock/core, atomic `SessionLifetime`, single-bound-scope or inline-close experiment is included.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	CI corrections retain their failed evidence: three local-feed repros now route through the dedicated proof workflow; an existing callback test now expects the documented immediate close refusal while still checking the injected I/O error and native exclusion. The previous Linux ARM64/net10 allocation assertion observed 6,056 bytes; **its cause remains unknown**. The test now isolates measurement on a dedicated thread, retains the exact zero-byte requirement and 1,000 measured publications, verifies state publication, and includes an escaping-allocation control that fails the zero assertion at 24,000 bytes. Local x64 results do not qualify ARM64; the final ARM64 job must pass. No measured retry/discard or threshold relaxation is used.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The original eight enumerated production findings have fixes and permanent guards: Shared-close admission cancellation; maintenance fairness; raw-close waiter cancellation; nested Shared-owner begin rejection; disposed-child validation before abort handling; disposal racing session close; peer-fatal versus genuine cleanup errors; and stale-sort cleanup under writable ownership. Subsequent review found and fixed deferred-fatal, child capture/admission and scope-handoff variants. All original failures and mutation controls remain in the linked audit, including failed intermediate fixes and harness corrections.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The [follow-up review](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/pull/133#pullrequestreview-5364329616) and [additional findings](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/pull/133#issuecomment-5908951510) have these dispositions:
