Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The newest review is addressed by checking all executing mutex-dependent cores before close changes state, preserving native protection when teardown is refused, and draining operations outside the Shared lock needed by callbacks. A closing-core fence prevents fresh opens from using a draining core. Forced owner-exit, pin, unleased-snapshot and fresh-open interleavings include plain/encrypted native exclusion, external writer progress and cold indexed/sentinel checks. Facade disposal from an owned ordinary-reader callback now refuses immediately; independent leased readers and non-owning facades retain their valid behavior. The guide also identifies the last ordinary reader's `Dispose()` as an idle pin-close error path. Actual before/fixed proofs retain the original failures, intermediate race and corrected plain/encrypted harness conditions.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	Public compatibility: typed/BSON collections, Include/vector queries, indexes and `$cols`/`$indexes` metadata are bound; read-only handles cannot write. SQL, FileStorage and maintenance are not handle APIs; drop/rename and external/system query I/O reject. Unsupported custom/coordinated/decorated providers and Shared private storage/caller streams reject handles; Direct caller streams remain supported. Existing interface implementers gain no required members. Legacy signatures remain with CS0618 warnings and a documented warnings-as-errors migration. Known committed outcomes survive cleanup errors; disposed facade access throws `ObjectDisposedException`. Parameterless Shared begin remains unbounded; opt-in timeout/cancellation bounds begin admission separately from collection `TIMEOUT`.
